window.OSAP_FT=window.OSAP_FT||{};window.OSAP_FT["21"]={"https://theconversation.com/more-than-half-of-those-aligned-with-sovereign-citizen-movement-think-violence-may-be-needed-296270":{"r":"conversation","at":"2026-11-17T16:16Z","sha":"1142c09e434dbcbb0ce1ff5c2009b116b4e5e8df5524184cc7d6094c57eabcc8","read":"https://theconversation.com/more-than-half-of-those-aligned-with-sovereign-citizen-movement-think-violence-may-be-needed-296270","bytes":179109,"x":"fulltext v1","title":"More than half of those aligned with sovereign citizen movement think violence may be needed","by":[{"n":"Michelle Grattan","r":"Professorial Fellow, University of Canberra"}],"pub":"2026-11-13T06:02:22Z","mod":"","blocks":[{"k":"p","s":["A new study has found more than a third of Australians who identify with the sovereign citizen movement support violent extremism."]},{"k":"p","s":["Further, more than half think violence may be needed to combat perceived injustice."]},{"k":"p","s":["The Australian Institute of Criminology – a government national research and knowledge centre on crime and justice – ",["has built a profile of people aligned with the sovereign citizen movement.","https://www.aic.gov.au/publications/tandi/tandi738"]]},{"k":"p","s":["Public attention focused on sovereign citizens after Dezi Freeman shot dead two policemen in north eastern Victoria in August last year. Freeman was killed in a police raid near the the Victorian-New South Wales border in March."]},{"k":"p","s":["The institute’s work is based on a survey of 9951 Australians in late 2024, in which 1.8% identified with and held beliefs aligned with the moment."]},{"k":"p","s":["Those respondents were a diverse group. They were more likely than other respondents to be male and under 35 years, to have had contact with the criminal justice system for violent offences, to own firearms, to engage in frequent religious observance and to have changed religion, and to be serving in the Australian military."]},{"k":"p","s":["They were also more likely to report various negative life events, to engage in conspiratorial thinking, to hold strong anti-government and other extreme views, to have misogynistic attitudes and to support extremist violence."]},{"k":"h","s":["Profile of people aligned with sovereign citizen movement"]},{"k":"p","s":["Those aligned with sovereign citizenry were also more likely to be dissatisfied with democracy and to think votes were never counted fairly in elections, as well as believing there was a high degree of political corruption."]},{"k":"p","s":["While most people in the survey reported having at least one negative event in prior months, people aligned with the sovereign citizen movement reported having had a range of recent such events or changes. These included increased financial stress."]},{"k":"p","s":["They also often exhibited problematic online behaviour."]},{"k":"h","s":["Support for the use of violence"]},{"k":"p","s":["Efforts to reduce the risk associated with the sovereign citizen movement must consider how to reduce the availability and attractiveness of online misinformation and propaganda that perpetuates the ideology, and reduce the sharing of narratives and tactics on different platforms that enables the movement to expand globally."]},{"k":"p","s":["Those aligned with the sovereign citizen movement had “overlapping and intersecting ideologies”."]},{"k":"p","s":["When asked where they sat on the political spectrum they were more likely to indicate they sat right of centre."]},{"k":"p","s":["But asked about attitudes associated with left-wing and right-wing radicalisation, they were more likely than other people to agree with both. “Many who held strong right-wing views also agreed with strong left-wing views.”"]},{"k":"p","s":["This may reflect the contemporary radicalisation threat environment, where a growing number of people have extreme views underpinned by mixed ideologies."]},{"k":"p","s":["However, their views on group dominance and inequality—along with views typically associated with populist right-wing movements, particularly anti-immigration stances—are consistent with the white nationalist views that formed the basis of the movement in the United States."]},{"k":"p","s":["For this reason, there have been signs of the sovereign citizen movement increasingly converging with the far-right movement in Australia. Conversely, the views in line with left-wing radicalisation are also compatible with their anti-authority and anti‑government ideals. This highlights the diversity of views that actually exist among individuals aligned with this movement, and the challenges associated with responding."]}],"chars":3552},"https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/belarus":{"r":"govuk","at":"2026-11-17T16:30Z","sha":"e0bdaf002899574d02ff5795e08f609a80814f6cd34c31f5e5ce7e878103c6a2","read":"https://www.gov.uk/api/content/foreign-travel-advice/belarus","bytes":68334,"x":"fulltext v1","title":"Belarus travel advice","by":[{"n":"Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office","r":""}],"pub":"2013-03-14T00:00:00+00:00","mod":"2026-09-10T14:40:11+01:00","blocks":[{"k":"h","s":["Warnings and insurance"]},{"k":"p","s":["Your travel insurance could be invalidated if you travel against advice from the Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office (FCDO)."]},{"k":"h","s":["FCDO advises against all travel to Belarus"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO advises against all travel to Belarus. You face a significant risk of arrest if you have at any time engaged in any activity now considered illegal by the Belarusian regime."]},{"k":"p","s":["There is also a low risk that direct conflict linked to the war in Ukraine may spread to Belarus. Find out ",["more about why FCDO advises against all travel","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/belarus/safety-and-security#risk-of-arrest-and-detention"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Limited UK government support"]},{"k":"p","s":["In the unlikely event that conflict starts, FCDO’s ability to support British nationals will be severely limited."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Dual nationals"]},{"k":"p","s":["Belarus does not recognise dual nationality. If you have both Belarusian and British passports, the authorities will consider you Belarusian, even if you enter the country on your British passport. Dual British-Belarusian nationality will not reduce your risk of arrest, questioning, or detention if you have engaged in any political activity related to Belarus. This limits the UK government’s ability to help you. See ",["Safety and security","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/belarus/safety-and-security#dual-nationals"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Travel insurance"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you choose to travel against FCDO advice, research your destinations and ",["get appropriate travel insurance","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/foreign-travel-insurance"],". Insurance should cover your itinerary, planned activities and potential expenses in an emergency."]},{"k":"h","s":["About FCDO travel advice"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO provides advice about risks of travel to help you make informed decisions. Find out ",["more about FCDO travel advice","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/about-foreign-commonwealth-development-office-travel-advice"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Get travel advice updates"]},{"k":"p","s":["Sign up to ",["get email notifications","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/belarus/email-signup"]," when this advice is updated."]},{"k":"p","s":["Follow FCDO:"]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on X","https://x.com/fcdotravelgovuk"]]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on Facebook","https://www.facebook.com/FCDOTravel/"]]},{"k":"li","s":[["Travel Aware on Instagram","https://www.instagram.com/travelaware/"]]},{"k":"h","s":["Entry requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["This information is for people travelling on a full ",["‘British citizen’ passport","https://www.gov.uk/types-of-british-nationality"]," from the UK who choose to travel against FCDO advice. It is based on the UK government’s understanding of the current rules for the most common types of travel."]},{"k":"p","s":["The authorities in Belarus set and enforce entry rules. If you’re not sure how Belarus’s entry requirements apply to you, contact the ",["Embassy of Belarus in the UK","https://uk.mfa.gov.by/en/embassy/address/"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Passport validity requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["To visit Belarus, your passport must be valid for at least 3 months from the date you arrive."]},{"k":"p","s":["Check with your travel provider to make sure your passport and other travel documents meet their requirements. ",["Renew your passport","https://www.gov.uk/renew-adult-passport/renew"]," if you need to."]},{"k":"h","s":["Dual nationals"]},{"k":"p","s":["When returning to the UK, British citizens must carry a valid ",["British passport","https://www.gov.uk/browse/abroad/passports"]," or ",["certificate of entitlement","https://www.gov.uk/right-of-abode/apply-for-a-certificate-of-entitlement"],". See ",["Dual Citizenship","https://www.gov.uk/dual-citizenship"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Children of Belarusian passport holders"]},{"k":"p","s":["Children aged 15 and under who do not have a Belarusian passport but have a parent who does, must have a travel certificate issued by a Belarusian embassy or consulate to enter Belarus. Parents of children entering on this document should contact the State Border Committee, Ministry of Interior and Ministry of Foreign Affairs to make sure their children also have the relevant documentation to exit Belarus."]},{"k":"h","s":["Visa requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["The Belarusian authorities have announced that until 31 December 2026 all British nationals (except those entering on a diplomatic or official passport) can enter Belarus for up to 30 days without a visa, limited to a total of 90 days per calendar year."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you want to stay longer than 30 days during a visit, or for a total period exceeding 90 days per calendar year, you must have a visa. You can find more information from the ",["Embassy of Belarus in the UK","https://uk.mfa.gov.by/en/embassy/address/"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you’re travelling directly from or to Russia, the visa-free arrangement does not apply and you must have a visa. As of 11 January 2025, foreign citizens with a Belarusian or Russian visa may enter, leave, stay or transit through either Belarus or Russia during the period of validity of the visa. This is limited to certain international border crossing points. For more information, please see the website of the ",["Belarusian Ministry of Foreign Affairs","https://mfa.gov.by/en/press/news_mfa/e90f53f504e4694f.html"]]},{"k":"hs","s":["Visas for travelling through Belarus"]},{"k":"p","s":["You do not need a visa to transit Belarus by air, but the maximum period you can remain airside (in the airport without going through border control) is 24 hours."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Travelling by air between Belarus and Russia"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO is not aware of any difficulties for British nationals travelling by air between Belarus and Russia. However, make sure you get an exit stamp in your passport when you leave Belarus. If you have no stamp, you will not be allowed back in on your next visit."]},{"k":"h","s":["Land borders"]},{"k":"p","s":["The land border with Ukraine is currently closed to British nationals. The border with Poland reopened on 25 September 2025. Other land borders have restrictions. See ",["Safety and security","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/belarus/safety-and-security"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Vaccine requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["For details about medical entry requirements and recommended vaccinations, see ",["TravelHealthPro’s Belarus guide","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/country/24/belarus#Vaccine_Recommendations"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Insurance requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["When you apply for your visa or when you arrive in Belarus, you will need to show both:"]},{"k":"li","s":["documentary evidence of medical insurance to the value of 10,000 euros"]},{"k":"li","s":["funds equivalent to 25 euros per day"]},{"k":"p","s":["This can be in the form of cash, credit card or travellers cheques in any currency. Contact the ",["Embassy of Belarus","https://uk.mfa.gov.by/en/embassy/address/"]," for full details."]},{"k":"h","s":["Journalists"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you plan to work as a journalist in Belarus, you must get accreditation from the Ministry of Foreign Affairs of Belarus before you travel."]},{"k":"h","s":["Registering with the authorities"]},{"k":"p","s":["Accommodation providers must register any foreign guests staying for more than 10 days in Belarus. Registration will normally be arranged by your hotel. If you’re staying with someone, check that they’re doing this. You can ",["register with the authorities online","https://portal.gov.by/PortalGovBy"]," (this page may not be accessible from all countries) or with the local police office (OVIR) in the district where you’re staying. There are fines for not registering in time. See the ",["Ministry of Foreign Affairs website","https://mfa.gov.by/en/visa/registration/"]," for more details."]},{"k":"h","s":["Customs rules"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you are carrying cash or travellers cheques worth more than 10,000 US dollars, you must complete a currency declaration form and get it stamped by a customs officer."]},{"k":"p","s":["You are not allowed to take more currency out of Belarus than you declared when you arrived. Keep your entry declaration form for the duration of your visit. When you leave Belarus, you must complete a new form and show the entry form. If you do not, you could be delayed and fined."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Importing goods for charitable purposes"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you want to import goods or services for charitable purposes, consult the ",["Embassy of Belarus in the UK","https://uk.mfa.gov.by/en/embassy/address/"]," for advice about local procedures and tariffs. If you think your goods will be exempt from Belarusian taxation, get written confirmation from the embassy."]},{"k":"h","s":["Safety and security"]},{"k":"h","s":["Terrorism"]},{"k":"p","s":["There is a high threat of terrorist attack globally affecting UK interests and British nationals, including from groups and individuals who view the UK and British nationals as targets. Stay aware of your surroundings at all times."]},{"k":"p","s":["UK Counter Terrorism Policing has information and advice on ",["staying safe","https://www.counterterrorism.police.uk/runhidetell/"]," and what to do in the event of a terrorist attack. Find out ",["how to reduce your risk from terrorism while abroad","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/reduce-your-risk-from-terrorism-while-abroad"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Terrorism in Belarus"]},{"k":"p","s":["Although there’s no recent history of terrorism in Belarus, attacks cannot be ruled out."]},{"k":"p","s":["Attacks could happen anywhere and could affect western interests and places visited by foreign nationals."]},{"k":"h","s":["Political situation"]},{"k":"p","s":["Demonstrations and protests are possible and could become confrontational, with security forces likely to use force to disperse and detain demonstrators. The authorities launched a violent crackdown against demonstrators across Belarus after the 2020 presidential elections."]},{"k":"p","s":["Belarus is still a repressive environment. You should:"]},{"k":"li","s":["stay vigilant"]},{"k":"li","s":["avoid crowds"]},{"k":"li","s":["if protests break out, leave the area immediately"]},{"k":"p","s":["There is often a heightened security presence, including on the streets of Minsk and other major cities. Security forces (some may be in plain clothes) might carry out a stop and search or ask for identification documents, and require you to unlock your phone. Make sure you carry your passport or a printed copy of the photo page of your passport, and visa if you have one."]},{"k":"h","s":["Risk of arrest and detention"]},{"k":"p","s":["You face a significant risk of arrest, questioning or detention if you have engaged in any political activity related to Belarus. This can include having donated to organisations or supported activity now considered illegal by the Belarusian regime. Laws can be applied retroactively by the authorities."]},{"k":"p","s":["If the authorities suspect you of engaging in and supporting activities against Belarusian law (including political activities), even if activities took place outside Belarus, you may be subject to:"]},{"k":"li","s":["deportation"]},{"k":"li","s":["detention"]},{"k":"li","s":["investigation"]},{"k":"li","s":["prosecution"]},{"k":"p","s":["The risk of arrest and detention is greater for those, including for dual nationals, who have previously been involved in activities inside or outside of Belarus, or on social media, which the authorities have since classed as illegal."]},{"k":"p","s":["Mobile phones and other personal items may be seized and inspected by the authorities, including at the border upon entering Belarus."]},{"k":"p","s":["Belarus has designated BBC News Russian content as “extremist material” under BBC.com. Distributing, reposting or storing material from BBC.com is now an offence under Belarusian law."]},{"k":"h","s":["Dual nationals"]},{"k":"p","s":["Belarus does not recognise dual nationality. Even if you have both Belarusian and British passports, the authorities will consider you Belarusian. Dual British-Belarusian nationality will not reduce your risk of arrest, questioning, or detention if you have engaged in any political activity related to Belarus. In the event of arrest or detention, Belarusian authorities are unlikely to allow FCDO consular access. This limits the UK government’s ability to help you."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Military service"]},{"k":"p","s":["In Belarus, military service is compulsory for all males between the ages of 18 and 27. Dual UK-Belarus nationals in that age group may be liable for military service and should check before travelling to Belarus."]},{"k":"h","s":["Russian military activity"]},{"k":"p","s":["The Belarusian regime is facilitating Russia’s war against Ukraine. Russian military activity is taking place inside Belarus. The situation is unpredictable and there is a low risk that direct conflict linked to the war in Ukraine may spread to Belarus. In the unlikely event that a conflict starts, FCDO’s ability to support British nationals may be limited. Events in Ukraine are fast-moving."]},{"k":"p","s":["There is a higher risk of military activity in places where there are Russian and Belarusian military personnel and equipment, including:"]},{"k":"li","s":["Mazyr/Mozyr (Gomel Oblast)"]},{"k":"li","s":["Zyabrovka (Gomel Oblast)"]},{"k":"li","s":["Vileyka / Viliejka (Minsk Oblast)"]},{"k":"li","s":["Gantsevichi / Hantsavichy (Brest Oblast)"]},{"k":"li","s":["Osipovichi/ Asipovichy (Mogilev Oblast)"]},{"k":"li","s":["Lida (Grodno Oblast)"]},{"k":"p","s":["Avoid restricted areas reserved for military exercises or operations. It’s illegal to take photographs of police or military personnel, or locations of strategic importance, including government buildings and critical infrastructure. There may not be warning signs in locations where prohibitions are in place. If in doubt, leave potentially restricted areas immediately."]},{"k":"p","s":["Monitor local media, sign up to ",["get email notifications","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/belarus/email-signup"]," when this advice is updated, and read our advice on how to ",["deal with a crisis overseas","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/how-to-deal-with-a-crisis-overseas"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Crime"]},{"k":"p","s":["Crime levels are low in Belarus, but be alert to the possibility of street crime and theft from vehicles or hotel rooms."]},{"k":"h","s":["Border crossings"]},{"k":"p","s":["There may be long queues at border crossing points and there are few facilities for those waiting. Customs and immigration can be lengthy and bureaucratic."]},{"k":"p","s":["If driving, you must be able to produce original vehicle ownership documents at border crossings. If you’re not the owner, you will need a letter to show you have ‘power of attorney’. You must have car insurance. See ",["Road travel","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/belarus/safety-and-security#road-travel"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you overstay or break the temporary import terms and conditions, your vehicle could be confiscated at the border or at an in-country police checkpoint."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Belarus-Russia border"]},{"k":"p","s":["See FCDO’s ",["Russia travel advice","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/russia"]," if considering travel to or from Russia."]},{"k":"p","s":["As of 11 January 2025, foreign citizens with a Belarusian or Russian visa may enter, leave, stay or transit through either Belarus or Russia during the period of validity of the visa. This is limited to certain international border crossing points. For more information, please see the website of the ",["Belarusian Ministry of Foreign Affairs","https://mfa.gov.by/en/press/news_mfa/e90f53f504e4694f.html"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you do not have a Belarusian or Russian visa, there are no legal grounds for foreigners (including British nationals) to cross the Russia-Belarus state land border including by road, bus or rail."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you travelled to Belarus from Russia and need assistance, contact the British Embassy Minsk on +375 17 229 8200 or send an enquiry using the ",["web contact form","https://www.contact.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/posts/belarus/british-embassy-minsk"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Belarus-Ukraine border"]},{"k":"p","s":["See FCDO’s ",["Ukraine travel advice","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/ukraine"]," if considering travel to or from Ukraine."]},{"k":"p","s":["British nationals cannot currently cross the Belarus-Ukraine border. If you travelled to Belarus from Ukraine and need assistance, contact the British Embassy Minsk on +375 17 229 8200 or send an enquiry using the ",["web contact form","https://www.contact.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/posts/belarus/british-embassy-minsk"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Belarus-Poland border"]},{"k":"p","s":["The border with Poland reopened on 25 September 2025.Open border crossings are Brest-Terespol Kuznica Bialostocka – Bruzhi (passenger vehicles only) and Bobrovniki – Bierastavitsa (passenger vehicles and freight). Political relations between Belarus and Poland remain unstable and the border could be closed again at short notice. Check the latest information on the ",["Polish State Border Guard website","https://granica.gov.pl/przejscia.php?v=en"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Belarus-Lithuania border"]},{"k":"p","s":["On 20 November 2025, Lithuania reopened two border crossings with Belarus: Medininkai – Kamenny Log and Salcininkai – Benyakoni. Political relations between Belarus and Lithuania remain unstable and the border could be closed again at short notice. Check the latest information on the ",["Lithuanian Government website","https://vrm.lrv.lt/en/news/lithuanian-government-have-decided-to-close-salcininkai-and-medininkai-border-checkpoints-with-belarus-for-one-month-4zSU/"]," (only available in Lithuanian)."]},{"k":"p","s":["Lithuania denies entry to cars registered in Belarus, with certain exemptions. For further information on these restrictions, see ",["the Lithuanian Government website","https://muitine.lrv.lt/lt/naujienos/lengvuju-automobiliu-su-baltarusiskais-registracijos-numeriais-ivaziavimas-i-lietuva/"]," (only available in Lithuanian)."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Latvian border"]},{"k":"p","s":["Only the Paternieki-Grigorovshchina border crossing point between Latvia and Belarus remains open. On 16 July 2024, Latvia introduced further restrictions at the border with Belarus to deny entry to passenger vehicles registered in Belarus. This border crossing may close or implement restrictions at short notice. Check the latest information on the ",["Latvian State Border Guard website","https://www.rs.gov.lv/en"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Transport risks"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Road travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you are planning to drive in Belarus, see ",["information on driving abroad","https://www.gov.uk/driving-abroad"]," and check the ",["rules of the road in the RAC’s Belarus guide","https://www.rac.co.uk/drive/travel/country/belarus/"],". The guide lists driving regulations and other legal requirements you need to be aware of."]},{"k":"p","s":["You need car insurance to drive in Belarus. There is more information about ",["insurance requirements on the State Border Committee website","https://gpk.gov.by/en/border-crossing/"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["There is a zero-tolerance policy towards drink-driving. Depending on the situation, penalties can include fines or imprisonment."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Tolls"]},{"k":"p","s":["Drivers with foreign licence plates must pay a fee to use toll roads or face a fine. For more information, see the BelToll website."]},{"k":"p","s":["There can be police checkpoints on routes throughout the country. Stop when instructed and have vehicle documentation available."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Road conditions"]},{"k":"p","s":["Driving standards in Belarus are unpredictable and road conditions vary. M-class highways are in good condition. Some R-class roads are impassable for periods in winter. Roadworks and potholes are usually poorly marked."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Air travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["There are no direct flights between Belarus and the UK."]},{"k":"p","s":["Since mid-2021 all Belarusian airlines have been banned from flying into the UK. The EU has closed its airspace to Belarusian registered aircraft and has sanctioned Belavia, the Belarusian state-owned carrier."]},{"k":"p","s":["Due to international restrictions on Belavia, they may not have access to components which are needed for the safe maintenance of their aircraft."]},{"k":"p","s":["UK government staff do not use Belavia."]},{"k":"p","s":["The UK has placed sanctions on some Russian airlines, including Aeroflot, Rossiya Airlines and Ural. You can buy flight tickets if the journey is starting in, or within, Russia without breaching UK sanctions."]},{"k":"p","s":["The UK has also sanctioned the Belarusian air traffic control operator."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Rail travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["There are frequent standard security checks at some stations on the Minsk Metro. Transport police may ask to scan your bags. You must comply with this. You could be arrested if you refuse."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Jaywalking"]},{"k":"p","s":["Police can fine you if you are jaywalking – ignoring pedestrian crossings or signals."]},{"k":"h","s":["Chernobyl site"]},{"k":"p","s":["There’s an exclusion zone immediately around the Chernobyl site. This includes the area close to the border with Ukraine in the south-east of Belarus. Access to this part of the country is limited. The risk of radioactive contamination from the Chernobyl site is insignificant elsewhere."]},{"k":"h","s":["Laws and cultural differences"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Illegal drugs"]},{"k":"p","s":["There are severe penalties for drug-related crimes in Belarus. Possession of illegal drugs, including small amounts of Class C substances such as cannabis, can result in prison sentences of up to 5 years. More serious offences, such as smuggling or trafficking, carry penalties of up to 25 years in prison and heavy fines. These laws apply even if you are only transiting through the airport."]},{"k":"p","s":["Airports in Belarus have excellent technology and security for detecting illegal items. This is also used to scan the baggage of transiting passengers. You should expect strict enforcement and serious consequences if found in possession of illegal substances."]},{"k":"hs","s":["LGBT+ travellers"]},{"k":"p","s":["Same-sex sexual activity is legal in Belarus. However, Belarus is still a conservative society and the LGBT+ scene is very low profile. Very few LGBT+ people are open about their sexuality. Read more ",["advice for LGBT travellers","https://www.gov.uk/lesbian-gay-bisexual-and-transgender-foreign-travel-advice"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Surrogacy"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO advises against commissioning surrogacy arrangements in Belarus."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you are a British national with existing surrogacy arrangements in Belarus, contact FCDO on 01908 516 666 if you have not already done so."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you already have surrogacy arrangements in place:"]},{"k":"li","s":["You may wish to ask your surrogacy agency about the safest location where all parties to the surrogacy arrangement can stay. FCDO cannot facilitate evacuation."]},{"k":"li","s":["In many cases, Belarusian law requires that the surrogate is married. This could affect British nationality for a child born under such a surrogacy arrangement. You should seek specialist UK legal advice about nationality determinations, Parental Orders and what you need for travel documents."]},{"k":"li","s":["Seek in-country legal advice. This is important for issues like legalising UK documents, notarising Belarusian documents, document processing and the maximum period of stay the authorities will allow. Due to the security challenges, FCDO cannot confirm which lawyers are currently offering services. The surrogacy process in Belarus can be lengthy and complicated."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you are considering changing your surrogacy arrangements, or making a new arrangement in another country, read the guidance for ",["intended parents planning for international surrogacy","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/surrogacy-guidance-for-intended-parents-pre-surrogacy-pre-birth-and-post-birth/guide-3-for-intended-parents-return-to-the-uk"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Money"]},{"k":"p","s":["There are plenty of ATMs in major cities. However, there are currently problems withdrawing cash or paying with foreign credit and debit cards. Almost no major banks in Belarus accept foreign cards. Those banks that do may charge a higher commission."]},{"k":"p","s":["There is also limited cash available. Many ATMs have limits on the amount of cash you can withdraw, particularly for foreign cards. Not all cards will be accepted in Belarus. Many reputable international money transfer providers have suspended operations in Belarus. Consider taking cash in US dollars or euros."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Sanctions"]},{"k":"p","s":["The UK government has imposed ",["sanctions against a range of Belarusian companies","https://www.gov.uk/government/collections/uk-sanctions-on-the-republic-of-belarus"]," and individuals. These may impact on financial transactions."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Exchanging money"]},{"k":"p","s":["It can be difficult to exchange British pounds for Belarusian roubles. US dollars and euros are more widely accepted. Use government-licensed currency exchanges and have your passport with you. If you exchange money elsewhere, you could be fined or arrested and the currency will be confiscated."]},{"k":"p","s":["You cannot exchange Belarusian roubles into other currencies outside Belarus."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Bank services"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you’re travelling to Belarus, speak to your UK bank before you go. Because of sanctions against Belarus, your bank may block your card if you use it in Belarus."]},{"k":"h","s":["Health"]},{"k":"p","s":["Before you travel check that:"]},{"k":"li","s":["your destination can provide the healthcare you may need"]},{"k":"li","s":["you have ",["appropriate travel insurance","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/foreign-travel-insurance"]," for local treatment or unexpected medical evacuation"]},{"k":"p","s":["This is particularly important if you have a health condition or are pregnant."]},{"k":"h","s":["Emergency medical number"]},{"k":"p","s":["Dial 103 and ask for an ambulance."]},{"k":"p","s":["Contact your insurance or medical assistance company quickly if you’re referred to a medical facility for treatment."]},{"k":"p","s":["Most people in Belarus only speak Russian or Belarusian, so emergency services staff may not speak English. You may need to find someone who speaks English to interpret for you."]},{"k":"h","s":["Vaccine recommendations and health risks"]},{"k":"p","s":["At least 8 weeks before your trip:"]},{"k":"li","s":["check the latest ",["vaccine recommendations for Belarus","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/country/24/belarus#Vaccine_Recommendations"]]},{"k":"li","s":["see where to get vaccines and whether you have to pay on the ",["NHS travel vaccinations page","https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/travel-vaccinations/"]]},{"k":"p","s":["See what ",["health risks you’ll face in Belarus","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/country/24/belarus"],", including biting insects and ticks."]},{"k":"p","s":["Do not drink village well water. It is usually heavily contaminated with impurities. In cities, boil then filter tap water before drinking. Bottled water is widely available."]},{"k":"h","s":["Medication"]},{"k":"p","s":["The availability of local medical supplies cannot be guaranteed – bring all essential medication with you."]},{"k":"p","s":["The legal status and regulation of some medicines prescribed or bought in the UK can be different in other countries."]},{"k":"p","s":["TravelHealthPro explains ",["best practice when travelling with medicines","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/factsheet/43/medicines-abroad"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["For more information on the legal status of a specific medicine, contact the ",["Embassy of Belarus in the UK","https://uk.mfa.gov.by/en/embassy/address/"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Healthcare in Belarus"]},{"k":"p","s":["The standard of healthcare is below that of the UK."]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO has a list of ",["medical facilities in Belarus","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/belarus-list-of-medical-facilities"]," where some staff will speak English."]},{"k":"h","s":["Travel and mental health"]},{"k":"p","s":["Read ",["FCDO guidance on travel and mental health","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/foreign-travel-advice-for-people-with-mental-health-issues"],". There is also ",["mental health guidance on TravelHealthPro","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/factsheet/85/travelling-with-mental-health-conditions"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Getting help"]},{"k":"p","s":["The Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office (FCDO) cannot provide tailored advice for individual trips. Read this travel advice and carry out your own research before deciding whether to travel."]},{"k":"h","s":["Emergency services in Belarus"]},{"k":"p","s":["Ambulance: 103"]},{"k":"p","s":["Fire: 101"]},{"k":"p","s":["Police: 102"]},{"k":"h","s":["Contact your travel provider and insurer"]},{"k":"p","s":["Contact your travel provider and your insurer if you are involved in a serious incident or emergency abroad. They will tell you if they can help and what you need to do."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Refunds and changes to travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["For refunds or changes to travel, contact your travel provider. You may also be able to make a claim through insurance. However, insurers usually require you to talk to your travel provider first."]},{"k":"p","s":["Find out more about ",["changing or cancelling travel plans","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/about-foreign-commonwealth-development-office-travel-advice#changing-or-cancelling-travel-plans"],", including:"]},{"k":"li","s":["where to get advice if you are in a dispute with a provider"]},{"k":"li","s":["how to access previous versions of travel advice to support a claim"]},{"k":"h","s":["Support from FCDO"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO has guidance on staying safe and what to do if you need help or support abroad, including:"]},{"k":"li","s":["finding English-speaking ",["lawyers","https://find-a-professional-service-abroad.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/find/lawyers?country=Belarus"],", ",["funeral directors","https://find-a-professional-service-abroad.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/find/funeral-directors?country=Belarus"]," and ",["translators and interpreters","https://find-a-professional-service-abroad.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/find/translators-interpreters?country=Belarus"]," in Belarus"]},{"k":"li","s":["dealing with a ",["death in Belarus","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/belarus-bereavement-information/belarus-bereavement-information"]]},{"k":"li","s":["being ",["arrested in Belarus","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/belarus-prisoner-pack"]]},{"k":"li","s":["getting help if you’re a ",["victim of crime","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/victim-of-crime-abroad"]]},{"k":"li","s":["what to do if you’re ",["in hospital","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/in-hospital-abroad"]]},{"k":"li","s":["if you are ",["affected by a crisis","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/how-to-deal-with-a-crisis-overseas"],", such as a terrorist attack."]},{"k":"h","s":["Contacting FCDO"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Help abroad"]},{"k":"p","s":["See ",["how to get help from the UK government abroad","https://www.contact.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/posts/belarus/british-embassy-minsk"]," including in an emergency."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Help in the UK"]},{"k":"p","s":["You can call FCDO in London if you need urgent help because something has happened to a friend or relative abroad."]},{"k":"p","s":["Telephone: 020 7008 5000 (24 hours)"]},{"k":"p","s":[["Find out about call charges","https://www.gov.uk/call-charges"]]},{"k":"h","s":["Get travel advice updates"]},{"k":"p","s":["Sign up to ",["get email notifications","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/belarus/email-signup"]," when this travel advice is updated."]},{"k":"p","s":["Follow FCDO:"]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on X","https://x.com/fcdotravelgovuk"]]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on Facebook","https://www.facebook.com/FCDOTravel/"]]},{"k":"h","s":["Risk information for British companies"]},{"k":"p","s":["The Overseas Business Risk service offers ",["information and advice for British companies operating in Belarus","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/overseas-business-risk-belarus"]," on how to manage political, economic, and business security-related risks."]}],"chars":23778,"change":"Updated information on buying flight tickets if the journey is starting in, or within, Russia ('Safety and security’ page)"},"https://theconversation.com/domestic-abusers-dont-always-stop-in-the-home-partners-friends-and-family-can-become-victims-too-294807":{"r":"conversation","at":"2026-11-17T16:38Z","sha":"4c17f780176768a8ad37eccb0d5846102f319eb4384625c27c995763335f99b3","read":"https://theconversation.com/domestic-abusers-dont-always-stop-in-the-home-partners-friends-and-family-can-become-victims-too-294807","bytes":181198,"x":"fulltext v1","title":"Domestic abusers don’t always stop in the home – partners’ friends and family can become victims too","by":[{"n":"Morag Kennedy","r":"Senior Lecturer in Criminology and Criminal Justice, Nottingham Trent University"}],"pub":"2026-11-05T17:11:17Z","mod":"","blocks":[{"k":"p","s":["Intimate partner abuse occurs between two distinct parties: the victim and the perpetrator. But the harm done can be much more complicated than this. It often spills over to affect families, friends and many others in the victim’s life."]},{"k":"p","s":["Within the traditional ",["criminal justice system","https://theconversation.com/uk/search?q=criminal+justice+system"]," in the UK and beyond, this concept of two distinct parties is referred to as the ",["“victim-perpetrator dichotomy”","https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/epdf/10.1080/20504721.2017.1391000?needAccess=true"],". The majority of research on ",["intimate partner abuse","https://research-information.bris.ac.uk/ws/portalfiles/portal/106211196/Copy_of_manuscript_150217_Alison_Gregory_for_Pure.pdf"]," focuses on the primary victims of harm – the person who is suffering at the hands of their partner or spouse. Many of these primary victims will seek ",["support from family","https://bmjopen.bmj.com/content/bmjopen/7/3/e014511.full.pdf"]," and friends."]},{"k":"p","s":["These “informal supporters” can also include colleagues and neighbours. And given that intimate partner abuse affects ",["one in four women","https://refuge.org.uk/what-is-domestic-abuse/the-facts/"]," in England and Wales ( ",["one in six men","https://www.ncdv.org.uk/male-survivors-of-domestic-abuse/"],"), it is reasonable to assume that millions of people are supporting relatives, friends or acquaintances through these experiences."]},{"k":"p","s":["Despite this, research on the “co-victims” of intimate partner abuse is limited, and this is even scarcer in cases that involve homicide. My research examines ",["co-victims of intimate partner homicide","https://www.emerald.com/sc/article-pdf/doi/10.1108/SC-04-2026-0028/11874745/sc-04-2026-0028en.pdf"]," – the people who have been bereaved by the killing. For every person who is killed by their intimate partner, around ",["six to ten family","https://www.jstor.org/stable/pdf/26638376.pdf?refreqid=fastly-default%3Af187bcc433daa802fca9e0bfe38326dd&ab_segments=&initiator=&acceptTC=1"]," members are likely to be affected by the death."]},{"k":"p","s":["Needless to say, losing a loved one to a violent unexplained death is incredibly difficult to process. The tragic events can also come with major life changes, such as ",["unexpected childcaring responsibilities","https://www.jstor.org/stable/pdf/26638376.pdf?refreqid=fastly-default%3Af187bcc433daa802fca9e0bfe38326dd&ab_segments=&initiator=&acceptTC=1"]," when the victim was a parent."]},{"k":"h","s":["What affected families say"]},{"k":"p","s":["My research focused on intimate partner homicide in England and Wales. Bereaved families have shared their stories of how the harms of the perpetrator can “overspill” and create further victims. This can be understood through the lens of ",["coercive control","https://www.routledge.com/Learning-from-Victims-Family-Narratives-The-Impact-of-Offline-and-Online-Harm-in-Cases-of-Intimate-Partner-Homicide/Kennedy/p/book/9781032734965"],", a well-established framework for understanding, identifying, evidencing and prosecuting both ",["intimate partner abuse","https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2021/17/contents"]," and homicide."]},{"k":"p","s":["It is made up of four elements – the possibility of physical violence, intimidation, isolation and control. I ",["found","https://www.routledge.com/Learning-from-Victims-Family-Narratives-The-Impact-of-Offline-and-Online-Harm-in-Cases-of-Intimate-Partner-Homicide/Kennedy/p/book/9781032734965"]," that intimidation tactics can spill over into families of victims long before the primary victim is killed by the perpetrator."]},{"k":"p","s":["These family members experience coercive control often at the same time as their loved one suffers it, and as such become victims of the same perpetrator. This can result in similar harms as the primary victim experiences, but may have a different outcome. In their case it could, for example, manifest in family tension rather than a physical attack."]},{"k":"p","s":["How co-victimisation works"]},{"k":"p","s":["As an academic who specialises in violence against women and girls, I interviewed eight families bereaved by intimate partner homicide to find out how intimidation tactics worked."]},{"k":"p","s":["Shena* whose daughter and first grandchild were killed in 2013, spoke of being victimised while supporting her daughter through an abusive relationship. She experienced death threats, threats of rape and was stalked by the perpetrator while he was also abusing her daughter."]},{"k":"p","s":["He told Jamielee* that he was going to rape me and put me in hospital … she’d be watching him switch off my life support."]},{"k":"p","s":["In another example, Tam* and his daughter Sandy* experienced online gaslighting after the disappearance of Tam’s other daughter in 2013. ",["Gaslighting","https://theconversation.com/topics/gaslighting-56922"]," is a form of psychological manipulation that makes the victim or co-victim question their own reality."]},{"k":"p","s":["In a bid to reinforce his alibi, the perpetrator (an acquaintance) deceived Tam and Sandy into believing they were communicating with their missing loved one. Sandy contacted the perpetrator after her sister’s disappearance, but he lied about her whereabouts and even agreed to help the family to find her."]},{"k":"p","s":["But Sandy contacted him … on Facebook and he sent a message back, saying: ‘Oh I don’t know where she is, I had gone to bed. I was ill last night so I went to bed’ … He knew what was going to happen … he even put the rider in it to say, ‘I’ll get in touch with everybody I can to try and find out where she is’."]},{"k":"p","s":["These examples show that the ",["intimidation element","https://www.emerald.com/sc/article-pdf/doi/10.1108/SC-04-2026-0028/11874745/sc-04-2026-0028en.pdf"]," of coercive control can be used against co-victims of intimate partner homicide in the period before their loved one is killed."]},{"k":"p","s":["These co-victims – whether parents, siblings, friends or co-workers – fight to protect their loved one by arguing back and showing the perpetrator that his behaviour is not tolerated. However, there is a high chance that they could also put themselves in harm’s way simply by challenging the perpetrator’s behaviour and standing up for the victim."]},{"k":"p","s":["Co-victims’ experiences should be treated as legitimate and credible in their own right. After all, they are not solely witnesses to primary victims’ experiences."]},{"k":"p","s":["Doing so would provide a fuller understanding of coercive control, capture the wider impact of intimate partner homicide and generate more effective recommendations for prevention and support. This could improve criminal justice training on recognising and responding to the harm that co-victims experience."]},{"k":"p","s":["*Names have been changed."]}],"chars":5040},"https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/uzbekistan":{"r":"govuk","at":"2026-11-17T17:32Z","sha":"bb63fbcce2f1cf68e6b07c8704875313c80da2b24fcf22e54e6222f4e8880144","read":"https://www.gov.uk/api/content/foreign-travel-advice/uzbekistan","bytes":57297,"x":"fulltext v1","title":"Uzbekistan travel advice","by":[{"n":"Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office","r":""}],"pub":"2013-01-22T00:00:00+00:00","mod":"2026-03-19T14:06:30+00:00","blocks":[{"k":"h","s":["Warnings and insurance"]},{"k":"h","s":["Global travel impacts due to escalation in the Middle East"]},{"k":"p","s":["Escalation in the Middle East has caused widespread travel disruption, including airspace closures, delayed and cancelled flights."]},{"k":"p","s":["Your travel plans may be affected, even if your destination is not in the Middle East. Before you travel:"]},{"k":"li","s":["check travel advice for any countries or territories you are transiting through"]},{"k":"li","s":["check for the latest updates from your airline or tour operator"]},{"k":"li","s":["review your travel insurance policy for coverage"]},{"k":"li","s":["monitor local and international media for the latest information and sign up for travel advice email alerts"]},{"k":"h","s":["Before you travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["No travel can be guaranteed safe. Read all the advice in this guide as well as ",["support for British nationals abroad","https://www.gov.uk/government/collections/support-for-british-nationals-abroad"]," which includes:"]},{"k":"li","s":["advice on preparing for travel abroad and reducing risks"]},{"k":"li","s":["information for women, LGBT+ and disabled travellers"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Travel insurance"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you choose to travel, research your destinations and ",["get appropriate travel insurance","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/foreign-travel-insurance"],". Insurance should cover your itinerary, planned activities and expenses in an emergency."]},{"k":"h","s":["About FCDO travel advice"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO provides advice about risks of travel to help you make informed decisions. Find out ",["more about FCDO travel advice","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/about-foreign-commonwealth-development-office-travel-advice"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Get travel advice updates"]},{"k":"p","s":["Sign up to ",["get email notifications","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/uzbekistan/email-signup"]," when this travel advice is updated."]},{"k":"p","s":["Follow FCDO:"]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on X","https://x.com/fcdotravelgovuk"]]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on Facebook","https://www.facebook.com/FCDOTravel/"]]},{"k":"li","s":[["Travel Aware on Instagram","https://www.instagram.com/accounts/login/?next=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.instagram.com%2Ftravelaware%2F&is_from_rle"]]},{"k":"h","s":["Entry requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["This information is for people travelling on a full ",["‘British citizen’ passport","https://www.gov.uk/types-of-british-nationality"]," from the UK. It is based on the UK government’s understanding of Uzbekistan’s current rules for the most common types of travel."]},{"k":"p","s":["The authorities in Uzbekistan set and enforce entry rules. If you’re not sure how these requirements apply to you, contact the ",["Uzbek Embassy in the UK","https://www.uzembassy.uk/"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Passport validity requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["To enter Uzbekistan, your passport must have an ‘expiry date’ at least 3 months after the date you arrive."]},{"k":"p","s":["Check with your travel provider that your passport and other travel documents meet requirements. ",["Renew your passport","https://www.gov.uk/renew-adult-passport/renew"]," if you need to."]},{"k":"p","s":["You will be denied entry if you do not have a valid travel document, or try to use a passport that has been reported lost or stolen."]},{"k":"h","s":["Dual nationals"]},{"k":"p","s":["When returning to the UK, British citizens must carry a valid ",["British passport","https://www.gov.uk/browse/abroad/passports"]," or ",["certificate of entitlement","https://www.gov.uk/right-of-abode/apply-for-a-certificate-of-entitlement"],". See ",["Dual citizenship","https://www.gov.uk/dual-citizenship"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Uzbek-British dual nationals"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you enter Uzbekistan on an Uzbek passport and are also a British national, the British Embassy can only provide very limited assistance. Uzbekistan does not recognise dual nationality so will treat you as an Uzbek national only. If you’re arrested or detained, the authorities are unlikely to grant you access to British Embassy staff."]},{"k":"h","s":["Visa requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["You can visit Uzbekistan without a visa for tourism or business for up to 30 days."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you’re travelling for any other purpose, or intend to stay longer than 30 days, check the requirements with the ",["Uzbek Embassy in the UK","https://www.uzembassy.uk/"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you’re entering Uzbekistan on a visa, check it and carefully note the date of expiry and the number of entries permitted."]},{"k":"p","s":["Do not overstay your visa, or exceed your permitted duration of stay, even for a very short period. If you overstay, you could get a fine and possibly be deported. It is not always easy or possible to extend your stay or visa if you want to stay in the country for longer than planned. If you’re travelling overland, make sure you arrive at your intended border crossing in good time before your period of stay or visa expires."]},{"k":"p","s":["Make sure you get your passport stamped on entry."]},{"k":"p","s":["Border guards check the stamp on exit to make sure that travellers have not exceeded the 30-day period."]},{"k":"h","s":["Registering with the authorities"]},{"k":"p","s":["You must register with the local district OVIR (Department of Foreign Travel and Exit) within 3 days of your arrival. Saturdays and Sundays count as part of the 3-day period. If you travel to another city and plan to stay for longer than 3 days there, you’ll need to register again."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you’re staying at a hotel, the staff will register you. If you’re staying in a private house, your host should register you with their local district OVIR or on the electronic registration system, Emehmon. Tourist tax is usually included in the hotel bill, and private hosts pay the tourist tax on behalf of the guest."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you’re travelling in Uzbekistan in a camper van or staying in tents and camping, you must register yourself. Register online through the electronic registration system, Emehmon, within 3 days of arrival. When registering online, you’ll have to pay a tourist tax charge for each day of your stay. At the moment you can only pay the tourist tax online with an Uzbek debit card in local currency."]},{"k":"p","s":["The authorities will require a full record of where you’ve been in Uzbekistan upon departure. If you are unable to account for your whereabouts, or fail to get registered, you could get a fine for each day that is missing or late and possibly be deported."]},{"k":"h","s":["Vaccine requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["For details about medical entry requirements and recommended vaccinations, see ",["TravelHealthPro’s Uzbekistan guide","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/country/237/uzbekistan#Vaccine_recommendations"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Customs rules"]},{"k":"p","s":["There are strict rules about ",["goods you can take into or out of Uzbekistan","https://customs.uz/en/page/%D1%9Ezbekiston-respublikasi-hududiga-kirib-kelayotgan-zhismoniy-shahslar-uchun-bozhhona-qoidalari"]," (in Uzbek, Russian and English). You must declare anything that may be prohibited or subject to tax or duty. On entry to Uzbekistan, you will need to complete a customs declaration if you are bringing in either:"]},{"k":"li","s":["precious metals and gemstones in quantities over 65 grams"]},{"k":"li","s":["significant amounts of money, the threshold being 10,000 US dollars"]},{"k":"p","s":["You should fill in 2 copies of the customs declaration, and make sure customs officials sign and stamp both copies. You will need to show one copy at customs as you enter Uzbekistan and the other when you leave the country."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you carry currency or valuables that you did not declare on arrival, there is a risk that officials may confiscate them."]},{"k":"p","s":["You cannot leave Uzbekistan with more foreign currency than you brought with you."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Medication"]},{"k":"p","s":["Before you take any medication into Uzbekistan, check the ",["list of prohibited medicines and restricted medicines","https://customs.uz/en/page/ayrim-horizhiy-davlatlarning-zhismoniy-shahslar-uchun-zhoriy-qilingan-bozhhona-qoidalari"]," (in Uzbek and Russian)."]},{"k":"p","s":["Bring a doctor’s prescription if you travel with prescription medicines and declare them on your customs declaration form. Some non-prescription medicines in the UK cause problems on entry into Uzbekistan, including codeine. Possession can lead to civil or criminal proceedings if you have not declared them on arrival. Travellers at land borders are more likely to face problems."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Drones"]},{"k":"p","s":["The import and use of drones in Uzbekistan is largely prohibited, with some limited exceptions. You must get a licence to import drones and permission to use them before you arrive in Uzbekistan. If you import or use drones without permission, you can be fined or get a prison sentence. The authorities can also confiscate your equipment."]},{"k":"h","s":["Safety and security"]},{"k":"h","s":["Terrorism"]},{"k":"p","s":["There is a high threat of terrorist attack globally affecting UK interests and British nationals, including from groups and individuals who view the UK and British nationals as targets. Stay aware of your surroundings at all times."]},{"k":"p","s":["UK Counter Terrorism Policing has information and advice on ",["staying safe","https://www.counterterrorism.police.uk/runhidetell/"]," and what to do in the event of a terrorist attack. Find out ",["how to reduce your risk from terrorism while abroad","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/reduce-your-risk-from-terrorism-while-abroad"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Terrorism in Uzbekistan"]},{"k":"p","s":["Terrorist attacks in Uzbekistan cannot be ruled out."]},{"k":"p","s":["Attacks could be indiscriminate and happen anywhere. They could affect western interests and places visited by foreigners."]},{"k":"p","s":["Previous attacks include:"]},{"k":"li","s":["in 2022, rockets were fired from Afghanistan into southern Uzbekistan. Islamic State Khorasan Province (ISKP) claimed responsibility"]},{"k":"li","s":["in 2019, it was reported that 17 people were killed in an armed attack on a Tajik security checkpoint on the Uzbekistan-Tajikistan border. Islamic State Khorasan Province (ISKP) claimed responsibility"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you are travelling in areas where attacks have previously occurred, stay aware of your surroundings, keep up to date with local media reports and follow the advice of local authorities."]},{"k":"h","s":["Political situation"]},{"k":"p","s":["Avoid demonstrations or large gatherings of people, which are likely to attract a heavy presence from security forces. Use caution and maintain a high level of security awareness, particularly in public places."]},{"k":"h","s":["Crime"]},{"k":"p","s":["Pickpocketing and bag-snatching are common, especially in crowded places like bazaars and on public transport. Home burglaries and break-ins also happen, even in wealthier neighbourhoods."]},{"k":"p","s":["Use caution and take measures to reduce your personal risk, such as:"]},{"k":"li","s":["keep sight of your belongings at all times"]},{"k":"li","s":["be aware of thieves using distraction techniques"]},{"k":"li","s":["avoid carrying all your valuables together in one place e.g. handbags or pockets"]},{"k":"li","s":["exercise a similar level of caution as you would in the UK"]},{"k":"h","s":["Laws and cultural differences"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Personal ID"]},{"k":"p","s":["You must carry your passport with you at all times. This is a legal requirement in Uzbekistan, and the police often carry out checks. You can be fined or arrested for failing to do so."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Illegal drugs and prison sentences"]},{"k":"p","s":["Possession of drugs is illegal. You can get a long prison sentence if convicted. While prison conditions have reportedly improved, reports of widespread hepatitis and tuberculosis as well as allegations of mistreatment of some prisoners by officials remain a concern."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Alcohol laws"]},{"k":"p","s":["It is illegal to sell alcohol and tobacco to someone aged 20 or under."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Taking photos in secure areas"]},{"k":"p","s":["Any form of photography can upset the authorities, particularly photographs of government buildings. This includes the Palace of Forums in Tashkent and sometimes local bazaars not normally visited by tourists. While restrictions have been relaxed recently, you should check before taking photos, especially near airports, border checkpoints, military barracks, bridges, police stations and metro stations."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Prohibited material"]},{"k":"p","s":["It is prohibited to bring material into Uzbekistan that could be considered as undermining the state. This includes material which promotes war, terrorism, religious hatred or racism and pornographic content."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Social media"]},{"k":"p","s":["Be careful when posting images of your visit on social media, particularly in relation to images or comments about public buildings, the Uzbek flag, national food or customs. Disrespectful content, including criticism of the government, could result in fines or detention."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Ramadan"]},{"k":"p","s":["Ramadan is a holy month for Muslims. The dates vary by year and country. Cafés and restaurants may amend their menus and be busy around fast-breaking (‘Iftar’). Areas around mosques are also likely to be busy around prayer times. Take particular care not to offend religious sensitivities at this time."]},{"k":"hs","s":["LGBT+ travellers"]},{"k":"p","s":["Same-sex relationships are illegal, not recognised and still frowned upon socially. You should refrain from showing affection in public."]},{"k":"p","s":["Read more ",["advice for LGBT+ travellers","https://www.gov.uk/lesbian-gay-bisexual-and-transgender-foreign-travel-advice"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Money"]},{"k":"p","s":["The currency in Uzbekistan is the Uzbek so’m (or soum/som – UZS), and is not available in currency exchanges outside Uzbekistan."]},{"k":"p","s":["There are very few ATMs which accept international bank cards outside of the main cities. Bring enough cash for your entire stay, or withdraw cash from an ATM in a main city ahead of any planned travel to the regions. US dollars are the most easily exchanged foreign currency. Only change money through official exchange booths. It is illegal to change money on the black market, and there are harsh penalties. Official exchange booths will not accept damaged or marked bank notes, nor will they accept US dollars issued before 2013."]},{"k":"p","s":["Internationally issued credit cards are not widely accepted outside Tashkent and the main cities. Travellers cheques are not normally accepted."]},{"k":"h","s":["Transport risks"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Road travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you are planning to drive in Uzbekistan, see information on ",["driving abroad","https://www.gov.uk/driving-abroad"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["Foreign nationals can drive their own car into Uzbekistan without paying import duties and taxes for up to 90 days. After the 90 days, daily fees will be applied. You must declare the car at the point of entry. You must carry your vehicle registration document and insurance for driving in Uzbekistan."]},{"k":"p","s":["You can drive in Uzbekistan with a valid UK driving licence for 30 days. You’ll need to have both the ",["1968 version of the international driving permit (IDP)","https://www.gov.uk/driving-abroad/international-driving-permit"]," and your UK driving licence with you in the car. You cannot buy an IDP outside the UK, so get one before you travel."]},{"k":"p","s":["You should get your licence and IDP translated into either Russian or Uzbek to make interaction with local authorities and traffic police easier. This is not a legal requirement."]},{"k":"p","s":["Hire car companies often have stricter requirements for their customers, such as a year of driving experience, minimum age and holding an IDP."]},{"k":"p","s":["Uzbekistan road laws require you:"]},{"k":"li","s":["to drive on the right"]},{"k":"li","s":["to wear seatbelts for drivers and all passengers"]},{"k":"li","s":["not to use a mobile phone while driving"]},{"k":"p","s":["Roads often have poor surfaces and bad lighting. Driving standards are below those in the UK and can be erratic."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Taxis and buses"]},{"k":"p","s":["Use licensed taxis whenever possible - this includes:"]},{"k":"li","s":["official taxis at the airport"]},{"k":"li","s":["taxis booked using apps like Yandex or My Taxi"]},{"k":"p","s":["Unlicensed taxis are still common, despite licensing laws. Many taxis do not have rear seatbelts, so you may want to request a taxi that has them."]},{"k":"p","s":["Many buses and taxis in Uzbekistan run on compressed natural gas and safety regulations on these vehicles are often not followed. Where possible opt for modern vehicles."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Rail travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["Take similar precautions as you would in the UK. If you must travel overnight, keep valuables in a safe place. Do not leave your belongings in the compartment unattended. Lock the door from the inside. You’ll need to show your passport with a valid visa when booking train tickets."]},{"k":"h","s":["Extreme weather and natural disasters"]},{"k":"p","s":["Find out what you can do to ",["prepare for and respond to extreme weather and natural hazards","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/tropical-cyclones"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Earthquakes"]},{"k":"p","s":["Uzbekistan is in an active seismic zone. There are regular tremors."]},{"k":"p","s":["The US Federal Emergency Management Agency has advice about what to do before, during and after an earthquake."]},{"k":"h","s":["Regional risks"]},{"k":"p","s":["This section has safety advice for regions of Uzbekistan. It only covers regions where FCDO has specific advice."]},{"k":"p","s":["You should also read ",["FCDO’s overall travel advice","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/uzbekistan"]," and ",["safety and security advice","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/uzbekistan/safety-and-security"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Uzbekistan-Afghanistan border"]},{"k":"p","s":["Be cautious if travelling near the border with Afghanistan, and follow the instructions of the local authorities."]},{"k":"p","s":["The border crossing with Afghanistan, the Friendship Bridge, remains officially closed to foreigners. See ",["Afghanistan travel advice","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/afghanistan"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["The banks of the Amudarya river, which form the border between Uzbekistan and Afghanistan, are fenced off, and there is a significant Uzbek military presence within that area, known as the ‘neutral zone’. You risk arrest if you approach the fence or take photos of military infrastructure or personnel in the neutral zone."]},{"k":"p","s":["Many of the tourist sites close to the border require the permission of the local authorities to visit, or require you to be part of an organised tour group."]},{"k":"p","s":["In April 2022, rockets were fired from Afghanistan into southern Uzbekistan. Islamic State Khorasan Province (ISKP) claimed responsibility. See ",["Terrorism","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/uzbekistan/safety-and-security#terrorism"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Health"]},{"k":"p","s":["Before you travel check that:"]},{"k":"li","s":["your destination can provide the healthcare you may need"]},{"k":"li","s":["you have ",["appropriate travel insurance","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/foreign-travel-insurance"]," for local treatment or unexpected medical evacuation"]},{"k":"p","s":["This is particularly important if you have a health condition or are pregnant."]},{"k":"h","s":["Emergency medical number"]},{"k":"p","s":["Dial 103 and ask for an ambulance."]},{"k":"p","s":["Contact your insurance company quickly if you’re referred to a medical facility for treatment."]},{"k":"h","s":["Vaccine recommendations and health risks"]},{"k":"p","s":["At least 8 weeks before your trip:"]},{"k":"li","s":["check the latest ",["vaccine recommendations for Uzbekistan","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/country/237/uzbekistan#Vaccine_Recommendations"]]},{"k":"li","s":["see where to get vaccines and whether you have to pay on the ",["NHS travel vaccinations page","https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/travel-vaccinations/"]]},{"k":"p","s":["See ",["what health risks you’ll face in Uzbekistan","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/country/237/uzbekistan"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["Altitude sickness is a risk in parts of Uzbekistan. Read more about ",["altitude sickness on TravelHealthPro","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/factsheet/26/altitude-illness"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Air quality"]},{"k":"p","s":["Poor outdoor air quality is a risk in Uzbekistan, especially during winter between November and February. The major cities, including Tashkent and the Fergana valley, are most affected by these high seasonal levels of pollution."]},{"k":"p","s":["The air quality in Karakalkpakstan and Khorezm regions can be poor all year round, because of regular storms over the Aral Sea bringing salt, dust and pollutants into the air."]},{"k":"p","s":["Children, the elderly and those with pre-existing medical conditions may be especially affected."]},{"k":"p","s":["Read about the risks from ",["poor air quality, and steps you should take, on TravelHealthPro","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/disease/219/outdoor-air-quality"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Medication"]},{"k":"p","s":["The legal status and regulation of some medicines prescribed or bought in the UK can be different in other countries. See ",["Customs rules","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/uzbekistan/entry-requirements#customs-rules"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["Read ",["best practice when travelling with medicines on TravelHealthPro","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/factsheet/43/medicines-abroad"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Healthcare in Uzbekistan"]},{"k":"p","s":["The quality of medical care in Uzbekistan is generally poor. Most hospitals are badly equipped and unhygienic, with a limited supply of drugs. There is no guarantee that equipment will have been properly sterilised, especially in rural hospitals. You should avoid all but basic or essential treatment in the event of an emergency. Medical insurance companies usually refer complaints of a more serious nature to hospitals in Europe or New Delhi."]},{"k":"p","s":["There is no reciprocal healthcare agreement between the UK and Uzbekistan."]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO has a ",["list of medical providers in Uzbekistan","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/uzbekistan-list-of-medical-facilitiespractitioners/uzbekistan-medical-facilities"]," where some staff will speak English."]},{"k":"h","s":["Travel and mental health"]},{"k":"p","s":["Read ",["FCDO guidance on travel and mental health","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/foreign-travel-advice-for-people-with-mental-health-issues"],". There is also ",["mental health guidance on TravelHealthPro","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/factsheet/85/travelling-with-mental-health-conditions"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Getting help"]},{"k":"p","s":["The Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office (FCDO) cannot provide tailored advice for individual trips. Read this travel advice and carry out your own research before deciding whether to travel."]},{"k":"h","s":["Emergency services in Uzbekistan"]},{"k":"p","s":["Ambulance: 103"]},{"k":"p","s":["Fire: 101"]},{"k":"p","s":["Police: 102"]},{"k":"p","s":["A new unified emergency services number, 112, was introduced in 2024 to cover all emergency calls. The previous emergency numbers are still in operation."]},{"k":"p","s":["Uzbekistan also has a 24/7 tourist hotline, the “Call Centre for Safe Tourism”, on 1173, whose operators speak English as well as Uzbek and Russian, and can direct callers to locally available support."]},{"k":"h","s":["Contact your travel provider and insurer"]},{"k":"p","s":["Contact your travel provider and your insurer if you are involved in a serious incident or emergency abroad. They will tell you if they can help and what you need to do."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Refunds and changes to travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["For refunds or changes to travel, contact your travel provider. You may also be able to make a claim through insurance. However, insurers usually require you to talk to your travel provider first."]},{"k":"p","s":["Find out more about ",["changing or cancelling travel plans","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/about-foreign-commonwealth-development-office-travel-advice#changing-or-cancelling-travel-plans"],", including:"]},{"k":"li","s":["where to get advice if you are in a dispute with a provider"]},{"k":"li","s":["how to access previous versions of travel advice to support a claim"]},{"k":"h","s":["Support from FCDO"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO has guidance on staying safe and what to do if you need help or support abroad, including:"]},{"k":"li","s":["finding English-speaking ",["lawyers","https://find-a-professional-service-abroad.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/find/lawyers?country=Uzbekistan"],", ",["funeral directors","https://find-a-professional-service-abroad.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/find/funeral-directors?country=Uzbekistan"]," and ",["translators and interpreters","https://find-a-professional-service-abroad.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/find/translators-interpreters?country=Uzbekistan"]," in Uzbekistan"]},{"k":"li","s":["dealing with a ",["death in Uzbekistan","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/uzbekistan-bereavement-information/bereavement-information-uzbekistan"]]},{"k":"li","s":["being ",["arrested or imprisoned in Uzbekistan","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/uzbekistan-prisoner-pack/information-pack-for-british-nationals-arrested-or-detained-in-uzbekistan"]]},{"k":"li","s":["getting help if you’re a ",["victim of crime","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/victim-of-crime-abroad"]]},{"k":"li","s":["what to do if you’re ",["in hospital","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/in-hospital-abroad"]]},{"k":"li","s":["if you’re ",["affected by a crisis","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/how-to-deal-with-a-crisis-overseas"],", such as a terrorist attack"]},{"k":"h","s":["Contacting FCDO"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Help abroad"]},{"k":"p","s":["See ",["how to get help from the UK government abroad","https://www.contact.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/posts/uzbekistan/british-embassy-tashkent"]," including in an emergency."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Help in the UK"]},{"k":"p","s":["You can call FCDO in London if you need urgent help because something has happened to a friend or relative abroad."]},{"k":"p","s":["Telephone: +44 20 7008 5000 (24 hours)"]},{"k":"p","s":[["Find out about call charges","https://www.gov.uk/call-charges"]]},{"k":"h","s":["Get travel advice updates"]},{"k":"p","s":["Sign up to ",["get email notifications","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/uzbekistan/email-signup"]," when this travel advice is updated."]},{"k":"p","s":["Follow FCDO:"]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on X","https://x.com/fcdotravelgovuk"]]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on Facebook","https://www.facebook.com/FCDOTravel/"]]}],"chars":19411,"change":"Updated information about global travel impacts due to escalation in the Middle East (‘Warnings and insurance’ page)."},"https://theconversation.com/whats-the-new-advice-about-puberty-blockers-for-trans-and-gender-diverse-children-296699":{"r":"conversation","at":"2026-11-17T20:22Z","sha":"baf2d0394e96b8e86b9b5574d4222f4074eb8d201bc26971b84825d0a607027f","read":"https://theconversation.com/whats-the-new-advice-about-puberty-blockers-for-trans-and-gender-diverse-children-296699","bytes":186285,"x":"fulltext v1","title":"What’s the new advice about puberty blockers for trans and gender-diverse children?","by":[{"n":"Matthew Mitchell","r":"Lecturer in the School of Humanities and Social Sciences, Deakin University"}],"pub":"2026-11-17T02:47:09Z","mod":"","blocks":[{"k":"p","s":["Australia’s leading health research body yesterday released ",["draft advice","https://www.nhmrc.gov.au/sites/default/files/documents/attachments/gender-guidelines/FOR-CONSULTATION-Advice-on-Puberty-Suppressants.pdf"]," on puberty suppressants, also known as puberty blockers. These medicines should be available for some young people under 18 with “gender incongruence”, the National Health and Medical Research Council (NHMRC) says."]},{"k":"p","s":["The ",["review","https://www.nhmrc.gov.au/health-advice/guidelines-care-trans-and-gender-diverse-people"]," comes amid growing international efforts to restrict young people’s access to gender-affirming care."]},{"k":"p","s":["In January 2025, ",["Queensland","https://statements.qld.gov.au/statements/101903"]," stopped new patients in its public health system from starting puberty suppressants or gender-affirming hormones. The ",["NHMRC review","https://www.health.gov.au/ministers/the-hon-mark-butler-mp/media/minister-for-health-and-aged-care-second-press-conference-31-january-2025"]," was announced three days later."]},{"k":"p","s":["Restrictions have also been introduced or proposed in the ",["United Kingdom","https://www.gov.uk/government/news/ban-on-puberty-blockers-to-be-made-indefinite-on-experts-advice"],", ",["Finland","https://palveluvalikoima.fi/documents/1237350/22895008/Summary_minors_en.pdf/aaf9a6e7-b970-9de9-165c-abedfae46f2e/Summary_minors_en.pdf"],", ",["Sweden","https://www.socialstyrelsen.se/om-socialstyrelsen/pressrum/press/uppdaterade-rekommendationer-for-hormonbehandling-vid-konsdysfori-hos-unga/"],", ",["Argentina","https://www.argentina.gob.ar/normativa/nacional/decreto-62-2025-409265/texto"],", ",["Brazil","https://sistemas.cfm.org.br/normas/arquivos/resolucoes/BR/2025/2427_2025.pdf"],", ",["Turkey","https://www.hrw.org/news/2025/10/29/turkiye-draft-law-threatens-lgbt-people-with-prison"]," and ",["parts of Canada","https://www.alberta.ca/supporting-minors-to-make-informed-decisions"],". In the United States, ",["many states","https://www.kff.org/lgbtq/gender-affirming-care-policy-tracker/"]," have banned these treatments for minors. New Zealand introduced ",["restrictions in 2025","https://www.health.govt.nz/regulation-legislation/puberty-blockers"],", but its High Court overturned them last month."]},{"k":"p","s":["So, what is gender incongruence, what does the draft advice actually say, and why is there so much disagreement?"]},{"k":"h","s":["What are puberty suppressants and who are they for?"]},{"k":"p","s":["Puberty suppressants have been prescribed to ",["transgender and gender-diverse","https://humanrights.gov.au/resource-hub/by-resource-type/publications/sex-and-gender-rights/terminology"]," young people for ",["decades","https://doi.org/10.1080/00918369.2012.653300"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["They work by temporarily ",["suppressing the hormonal signals","https://www.nhmrc.gov.au/sites/default/files/documents/attachments/gender-guidelines/FOR-CONSULTATION-Advice-on-Puberty-Suppressants.pdf"]," that drive puberty, reducing the body’s production of oestrogen or testosterone."]},{"k":"p","s":["Puberty suppressants have been used to treat early (precocious) puberty ",["since the 1980s","https://doi.org/10.1056/NEJM198112243052602"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["Related medicines are also used to treat ",["endometriosis and some hormone-sensitive cancers","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK551662/"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["The new NHMRC advice concerns their use by young people experiencing ",["gender incongruence","https://www.who.int/standards/classifications/frequently-asked-questions/gender-incongruence-and-transgender-health-in-the-icd"],". This refers to a difference between a person’s gender and the sex recorded at birth. It’s not classified as a mental disorder."]},{"k":"p","s":[["Gender dysphoria","https://www.racgp.org.au/afp/2015/november/gender-dysphoria"],", by contrast, refers to the significant distress some people experience in relation to this incongruence."]},{"k":"p","s":["The aim of puberty suppression is to ",["delay unwanted pubertal changes","https://doi.org/10.1210/jc.2017-01658"]," that may cause distress, giving young people time to mature and consider their options."]},{"k":"p","s":["That’s why suppressants are often described as a ",["pause button","https://www.nhmrc.gov.au/sites/default/files/documents/attachments/gender-guidelines/FOR-CONSULTATION-Advice-on-Puberty-Suppressants.pdf"],". They delay potentially permanent changes, such as voice deepening or breast development."]},{"k":"p","s":["However, when treatment stops, hormonal signalling ",["usually resumes","https://doi.org/10.1542/peds.2008-1783"],", allowing puberty to progress."]},{"k":"p","s":[["Most young people","https://doi.org/10.1007/s10508-020-01660-8"]," who start suppressants later begin taking gender-affirming hormones (oestrogen or testosterone)."]},{"k":"p","s":["But some stop suppressants without starting hormones and their puberty resumes."]},{"k":"h","s":["How many young people actually receive these medicines?"]},{"k":"p","s":["Australia has ",["no national count","https://www.abc.net.au/news/2026-07-28/puberty-blockers-inside-the-fraught-debate/106914900"]," of young people receiving puberty suppressants."]},{"k":"p","s":["However, in July 2026, public health services reported ",["58 young people in Western Australia and 77 in Queensland","https://www.abc.net.au/news/2026-07-28/puberty-blockers-inside-the-fraught-debate/106914900"]," receiving suppressants."]},{"k":"p","s":["For perspective, the 135 patients in the WA and Queensland services represent about 0.007% of the ",["combined under-18 population","https://www.abs.gov.au/statistics/people/population/national-state-and-territory-population/jun-2025"]," of the two states."]},{"k":"h","s":["So why are puberty suppressants so controversial?"]},{"k":"p","s":["Like all medical treatments, puberty suppressants carry potential benefits and risks."]},{"k":"p","s":["The ",["NHMRC found","https://www.nhmrc.gov.au/sites/default/files/documents/attachments/gender-guidelines/Technical-Report.pdf"]," that while evidence about their long-term effects remains limited, the potential benefits likely outweigh the risks for some young people."]},{"k":"p","s":["However, in recent years, puberty suppressants have become a ",["flashpoint","https://www.penguin.com.au/books/whos-afraid-of-gender-9781802061062"]," in wider debates over transgender and gender-diverse people’s rights and identities."]},{"k":"p","s":["A growing international “ ",["anti-gender","https://doi.org/10.1086/701171"],"” movement, led largely by conservative, religious and right-wing groups, has portrayed puberty suppression as a threat to children’s welfare, parental authority, and traditional family structures."]},{"k":"p","s":["These groups have used this perceived threat to ",["win political support","https://doi.org/10.4324/9781003133520"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["Meanwhile, some feminist, gay, and lesbian critics argue gender-affirming care ",["reinforces gender stereotypes","https://doi.org/10.1177/0038026120934713"]," or amounts to ",["conversion therapy","https://doi.org/10.1080/19361653.2019.1665610"]," for young gay people."]},{"k":"p","s":["Their arguments often conflict with one another, but much of this opposition relies on false claims. These include claims that young people are routinely ",["rushed into treatment","https://www.nhmrc.gov.au/sites/default/files/documents/attachments/gender-guidelines/FOR-CONSULTATION-Advice-on-Puberty-Suppressants.pdf"]," or are ",["likely to regret it","https://doi.org/10.1080/26895269.2026.2709506"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["Many scholars argue the controversy is less about assessing medical evidence or safeguarding young people’s welfare. Instead, they see it as a proxy for broader ",["political conflicts over changing gender norms","https://doi.org/10.4324/9781003133520"]," and wider ",["social anxieties","https://www.penguin.com.au/books/whos-afraid-of-gender-9781802061062"]," about which genders, bodies, and ways of living society ought to support."]},{"k":"h","s":["What’s actually new about the NHMRC advice?"]},{"k":"p","s":["The NHMRC advice draws on a ",["systematic review","https://www.nhmrc.gov.au/sites/default/files/documents/attachments/gender-guidelines/Technical-Report.pdf"]," of the research, clinical expertise, and the experiences of young people and families."]},{"k":"p","s":["That review rated the overall evidence as “low certainty”, meaning the precise size of the potential benefits and risks remains uncertain. One reason is the absence of randomised trials, often considered the strongest way to test a treatment’s effects."]},{"k":"p","s":["But the review acknowledges that such trials would raise practical and ethical concerns in this context, and low certainty does not mean there is no evidence or that the medicines do not work."]},{"k":"p","s":["That’s why the draft advice ultimately supports young people and families to consider puberty suppression on a ",["case-by-case basis","https://www.nhmrc.gov.au/sites/default/files/documents/attachments/gender-guidelines/FOR-CONSULTATION-Advice-on-Puberty-Suppressants.pdf"],". It says young people, their parents or carers, and clinicians should be allowed to make this decision together."]},{"k":"p","s":["Importantly, the NHMRC also says treatment may be considered for young people with gender incongruence “ ",["with or without distress","https://www.nhmrc.gov.au/sites/default/files/documents/attachments/gender-guidelines/FOR-CONSULTATION-Advice-on-Puberty-Suppressants.pdf"],"”."]},{"k":"p","s":["This means young people need not experience distress or be diagnosed with a ",["mental disorder","https://www.who.int/standards/classifications/frequently-asked-questions/gender-incongruence-and-transgender-health-in-the-icd"]," before treatment can be considered."]},{"k":"p","s":["The underlying principle is that young people should not have to ",["suffer before receiving care","https://www.nhmrc.gov.au/sites/default/files/documents/attachments/gender-guidelines/FOR-CONSULTATION-Advice-on-Puberty-Suppressants.pdf"]," and may have legitimate reasons to seek treatment beyond relieving existing distress."]},{"k":"p","s":["The NHMRC advice therefore marks a significant departure from increasingly restrictive policies in other jurisdictions, such as England. There, the highly ",["contested","https://doi.org/10.5694/mja2.70035"]," 2024 Cass Review raised concerns about the strength of the evidence supporting puberty suppression. It recommended limiting treatment to research settings."]},{"k":"p","s":["The NHMRC committee, by contrast, concluded the benefits of puberty suppressants probably outweigh the risks for some young people, based on an ",["assessment","https://www.nhmrc.gov.au/sites/default/files/documents/attachments/gender-guidelines/Technical-Report.pdf"]," that acknowledges the limitations of the evidence, while also considering the consequences of restricting access to care."]},{"k":"h","s":["So, what happens now?"]},{"k":"p","s":["The advice is open for ",["public consultation","https://consultations.nhmrc.gov.au/clinical-practice-guidelines/advice-puberty-suppressants/"]," until January 18."]},{"k":"p","s":["The NHMRC will consider the feedback as it develops national clinical guidelines, due in ",["2028","https://www.nhmrc.gov.au/health-advice/guidelines-care-trans-and-gender-diverse-people/guideline-development"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["However, the NHMRC’s advice is ",["not legally binding","https://www.health.gov.au/ministers/the-hon-mark-butler-mp/media/statement-on-nhmrc-advice-on-puberty-suppressants?language=en"],". States and territories ",["decide","https://www.health.gov.au/ministers/the-hon-mark-butler-mp/media/health-care-for-trans-and-gender-diverse-australian-children-and-adolescents"]," how their public health services provide care."]},{"k":"p","s":["Restrictions remain in ",["Queensland","https://www.health.qld.gov.au/system-governance/legislation/ministerial-direction-treatment-of-gender-dysphoria-in-children-and-adolescents-with-hormone-therapy"],", where new patients under 18 cannot start treatment through public services."]},{"k":"p","s":["The ",["Northern Territory","https://territorystories.nt.gov.au/10070/1023410"]," also bans puberty suppressants in its public health system."]},{"k":"p","s":[["Both governments","https://www.abc.net.au/news/2026-11-16/puberty-blockers-interim-advice-health-medical-research-council/107376484"]," yesterday ruled out changes."]},{"k":"p","s":["The advice also leaves major ",["barriers to access","https://www.nhmrc.gov.au/sites/default/files/documents/attachments/gender-guidelines/Technical-Report.pdf"]," unresolved, including long waiting lists and treatment costs that can ",["exceed A$5,000 a year","https://www.nhmrc.gov.au/sites/default/files/documents/attachments/gender-guidelines/Technical-Report.pdf"],". For some families, these barriers make treatment inaccessible."]},{"k":"p","s":["Nevertheless, the draft provides a national basis for reconsidering restrictions and improving young people’s access to treatment."]}],"chars":7011},"https://theconversation.com/something-else-is-lurking-just-below-the-surface-of-newfoundlands-waters-raw-sewage-296015":{"r":"conversation","at":"2026-11-18T19:22Z","sha":"60e714b4e2c87d3133855b132d91789c948091b0828ba72c2818356d6a310124","read":"https://theconversation.com/something-else-is-lurking-just-below-the-surface-of-newfoundlands-waters-raw-sewage-296015","bytes":184735,"x":"fulltext v1","title":"Something else is lurking just ‘Below’ the surface of Newfoundland’s waters — raw sewage","by":[{"n":"Josie Wittmer","r":"Assistant Professor, Environment & Sustainability, Memorial University of Newfoundland"}],"pub":"2026-11-18T18:44:48Z","mod":"","blocks":[{"k":"p","s":["The popular new Netflix thriller series Below ",["portrays life in coastal Newfoundland and a frightening presence in the water","https://theconversation.com/what-lurks-below-newfoundland-waters-a-marine-biologist-explains-the-science-behind-the-show-295615"]," that turns out to be a giant squid. However, the show’s tagline, “something’s in the water,” also speaks to another alarming reality in the water for many Newfoundland communities: sewage."]},{"k":"p","s":["Municipalities across the island of Newfoundland struggle to manage and dispose of raw sewage. The persistence of sewage effluent in local waters is experienced as a ",["localized environmental threat, an infrastructural inequality, a burden on local finances and labour and a source of embarrassment","https://doi.org/10.1177/20438206261443545"]," in Newfoundland’s cities and towns that do not have the infrastructures to comply with federal wastewater management regulations."]},{"k":"p","s":["In a new ",["SSHRC-funded project","https://search.open.canada.ca/grants/record/sshrc-crsh,207-2026-2027-Q1-00694,current"],", I am exploring the mismatches between wastewater policies and local experiences of sewage infrastructure challenges in Newfoundland."]},{"k":"h","s":["Policy-infrastructure mismatches"]},{"k":"p","s":["In Canada, wastewater is regulated at the federal level under the Fisheries Act by the ",["Wastewater Systems Effluent Regulations (WSER)","https://www.canada.ca/en/environment-climate-change/services/wastewater/wastewater-systems-effluent-regulations.html"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["The regulations came into effect in 2015 and require secondary-level wastewater treatment (for example, facilities using biological processes to remove organic matter and suspended solids) to meet national quality standards and reduce threats to fish and human health."]},{"k":"p","s":["Yet, in Newfoundland and Labrador, ",["186 of 212 wastewater discharge points regulated by the WSER have no treatment at all","https://www.gov.nl.ca/eccc/files/11-WSER-ECCC-Presentation_NL-Workshop-2023-1.pdf"],". In 2021, only ",["three per cent of Newfoundland and Labrador wastewater systems complied with the WSER","https://www.gov.nl.ca/eccc/files/11-WSER-ECCC-Presentation_NL-Workshop-2023-1.pdf"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["Under the WSER, non-compliant municipalities must apply for “transitional authorizations (TAs),” or permissions to continue polluting. TAs require regular sampling, analyses in authorized labs, acute-lethality (fish kill) tests and the submission of this data to a federal portal."]},{"k":"p","s":["However, many smaller communities with few municipal staff and small budgets ",["struggle to finance, administer and monitor their water to meet these reporting requirements","https://municipalnl.ca/resolution/monitoring-burden-under-the-federal-wastewater-systems-effluent-regulations/"]," and continue polluting with permission."]},{"k":"h","s":["Infrastructural inequities"]},{"k":"p","s":["Newfoundland communities of various sizes struggle to bridge the gap between the enforcement of federal environmental policies and their sewage management realities on the ground."]},{"k":"p","s":["For example, the City of Corner Brook releases over 20,000 square metres (approximately eight Olympic-sized swimming pools) of raw, untreated sewage effluent into the ocean every day."]},{"k":"p","s":["The city has struggled for more than a decade to obtain provincial and federal funds to build a secondary treatment facility. The estimated cost of the facility required in Corner Brook is ",["$120-130 million","https://www.cbc.ca/news/canada/newfoundland-labrador/corner-brook-could-face-big-penalties-for-dumping-sewage-into-the-ocean-9.7134970"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["In early 2026, the ",["city was served with an environmental directive","https://www.cbc.ca/listen/live-radio/1-89-the-signal/clip/16203635-how-deal-sewage-messy-municipal-matters"]," by armed Department of Fisheries and Oceans officers who levied fines and threatened criminal charges because of the volume of effluent the city is releasing into the Bay of Islands."]},{"k":"p","s":["In the meantime, Corner Brook pays $60,000 a year to an authorized private lab to analyze their polluted water samples in order to provide the required data to the federal government’s portal."]},{"k":"p","s":["Small communities across the island are also dealing with big sewage issues. In addition to lacking wastewater treatment facilities, many municipalities have aging sewage infrastructure and experience frequent sewage blockages, line breaks and overflow."]},{"k":"p","s":["For small towns with an increased population during warmer months due to seasonal residents, workers and tourists, these challenges compound as the sewage increases. The town clerk of a community with around 1,000 residents recently estimated that 30 per cent of the town’s budget could be spent elsewhere ",["if not for the costs of water and sewage infrastructure repairs and wastewater monitoring","https://www.cbc.ca/listen/live-radio/1-89-the-signal/clip/16203635-how-deal-sewage-messy-municipal-matters"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Contracting out wastewater sampling"]},{"k":"p","s":["For many small municipalities, producing data to keep a transitional authorization can be a labour burden, logistically challenging and expensive. Third-party companies are stepping in to fill the gap between regulations and municipal realities by selling wastewater sampling, lab and consulting services for a fee."]},{"k":"p","s":["In a recent interview, an elected official in a small western Newfoundland town with a population of less than 500 residents told me: “When you see the (company) van drive through town, you know there’s also an $8,000 invoice coming.”"]},{"k":"p","s":["A 2019 report in The Telegram estimated that ",["80 per cent of Newfoundland municipalities contract out their wastewater sampling and analysis to one firm","https://www.saltwire.com/newfoundland-labrador/getting-ready-for-wastewater-treatment-315480#:%7E:text=Biomaxx%20opened%20a%20local%20office,and%20helping%20them%20work%20through"],", Biomaxx Environmental."]},{"k":"p","s":["Following ",["global trends in wastewater monitoring","https://doi.org/10.1177/23996544241313454"]," and the ",["growing presence of private sector solutions for municipal wastewater challenges","https://doi.org/10.1111/tran.12732"],", I suggest the current enforcement of WSER is creating the conditions for community wastewater challenges to become a source of profit."]},{"k":"p","s":["In other words, wastewater is emerging as a place where private companies are creating a market for themselves in municipalities struggling to comply with regulations that were not made with their infrastructure realities in mind."]},{"k":"h","s":["‘Get back from the water’"]},{"k":"p","s":["In Below, Josh Hartnett’s Calvin Penney runs through a community event shouting for everyone to “get back from the water!” When watching the scene, I was reminded of stories I’ve heard from municipal staff and residents of Newfoundland communities running up to tourists to tell them to stay out of the waters near outfalls, where pipes, drains or sewers discharge wastewater into bodies of water."]},{"k":"p","s":["One such incident included a cruise ship passenger clad in snorkel equipment, wading into the waters from the beach directly in front of Corner Brook’s largest sewage outfall."]},{"k":"p","s":["Untreated sewage — along with the disconnect between federal regulations and local infrastructure — is a major obstacle in communities that still lack wastewater treatment."]},{"k":"p","s":["Local sewage challenges also reach far beyond environmental pollution and municipal budgets, affecting community planning, tourism, public health, recreation, local food sovereignty and access to shoreline food sources."]}],"chars":5980},"https://theconversation.com/second-hand-solar-tiny-houses-no-bills-we-found-how-australians-are-going-off-grid-and-if-its-worth-it-296397":{"r":"conversation","at":"2026-11-19T05:03Z","sha":"2098d13cac5a7eea1463851e4b49e41a33266e80ddbd17f72b34b21c87e6b0fa","read":"https://theconversation.com/second-hand-solar-tiny-houses-no-bills-we-found-how-australians-are-going-off-grid-and-if-its-worth-it-296397","bytes":186991,"x":"fulltext v1","title":"Second-hand solar, tiny houses, no bills – we found how Australians are going off-grid and if it’s worth it","by":[{"n":"Peyman Akhgar","r":"Lecturer in Architecture, Griffith University"},{"n":"Anna Petrova","r":"Urban Ecologist, Griffith University"},{"n":"Mohammad Sanjari","r":"Senior Lecturer, Electrical Engineering, Griffith University"},{"n":"Sylvie Chell","r":"Lecturer in Ecological Engineering, Griffith University"}],"pub":"2026-11-19T03:51:27Z","mod":"","blocks":[{"k":"p","s":["As solar and batteries make it possible for homes to make and store their own power, more Australians are wondering whether they ",["need the power grid at all","https://www.sbs.com.au/news/insight/article/is-living-off-grid-actually-cheaper/659x148w4"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["Cost pressures and extreme weather are driving interest. Power prices rose five years in a row before ",["easing this July","https://www.aer.gov.au/industry/registers/resources/reviews/default-market-offer-prices-2026-27"],". One in five Australian households ",["is struggling","https://www.sbs.com.au/news/article/why-your-energy-bills-could-be-getting-higher-and-what-you-can-do-about-it/jofhpl9it"]," to pay its energy bills."]},{"k":"p","s":["Queensland is at the pointy end as the state most exposed to ",["extreme weather","https://www.climatecouncil.org.au/resources/state-of-queensland-disaster-ground-zero/"],". Just last week, a supercell storm left 82,000 residents ",["without power","https://www.energex.com.au/news/2026/seq-storms-power-restored-to-more-than-90-of-customers"],". Last year, ex-tropical Cyclone Alfred ",["left around 250,000","https://www.abc.net.au/news/2025-03-08/qld-homes-and-businesses-without-power-wind-rain-storm-weather/105026654"]," Queenslanders in the dark, some for ",["up to ten days","https://www.energex.com.au/news/2025/energex-restores-power-to-100-of-customers-after-Tropical-Cyclone-Alfred"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["Fully 25% of householders in Queensland plan to go off-grid at some point, according to the state’s ",["2026 energy survey","https://qhes.com.au/queensland-household-energy-survey-2026/going-off-grid-2026/"],", up from 22% a year earlier. Many have already gone off-grid, especially in regional areas where grid connections can cost tens of thousands of dollars."]},{"k":"p","s":["But we don’t know much about why Queenslanders make this choice. Once off grid, they ",["become invisible","https://doi.org/10.1016/j.enpol.2019.02.014"]," to official data records."]},{"k":"p","s":["In our ",["new research","https://doi.org/10.1016/j.erss.2026.105087"],", we interviewed off-grid Queenlanders to find out why people do it – and if it’s worth it."]},{"k":"h","s":["What do off-grid households look like?"]},{"k":"p","s":["Most off-grid households use solar panels to generate power and a home battery to store it. Some keep a backup generator for long cloudy periods."]},{"k":"p","s":["We surveyed 40 off-grid households, largely located in south-east Queensland. We interviewed ten householders in depth and spoke to four off-grid installers. We also asked utilities and eight councils what their rules say."]},{"k":"p","s":["Most respondents lived in rural or rural-residential areas. Their homes largely fell into one of two categories:"]},{"k":"p","s":["• Conventional houses (22 households). To go off-grid, the average house had 6.6 kilowatts of solar panels and a 40 kilowatt-hour battery, mostly put in by professional installers."]},{"k":"p","s":["• Tiny houses on wheels (ten households). These houses had much smaller off-grid systems, averaging about 3.5 kilowatts of solar and two 4 kWh batteries. Some owners installed these themselves and used a licensed electrician to check."]},{"k":"h","s":["Why do people do it?"]},{"k":"p","s":["The most common motivation was financial, listed by about 55% of survey respondents. But financial reasons meant different things for people in conventional homes and those in tiny homes."]},{"k":"p","s":["In conventional houses, going off-grid was mostly about avoiding the big cost of grid connection, which can be ",["$30,000 to $60,000","https://www.energymatters.com.au/renewable-news/off-grid-solar-system-cost-australia/"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["As one homeowner told us, off-grid meant:"]},{"k":"p","s":["an opportunity to save some money and work towards being self-reliant."]},{"k":"p","s":["By contrast, going off grid in a tiny house on wheels was seen as part of choosing to live simply. One resident used second-hand solar panels at about $70 per panel. They told us:"]},{"k":"p","s":["I lived in the suburbs, and you’re just getting bills for this, bills for that. [Now] I can buy some stuff, learn how it works, and kind of provide my own energy."]},{"k":"p","s":["Other motivations included care for the environment (25%), no grid access (20%), independence and control (12.5%) and finally doubts about grid reliability (10%)."]},{"k":"p","s":["The most common complaint (33% of people) was running short of power during long cloudy or rainy periods. But 20% saw no downsides at all."]},{"k":"p","s":["Many interviewees described feeling more independent and in control of their own energy supply."]},{"k":"h","s":["Was it worth it?"]},{"k":"p","s":["Off-grid living makes most sense where the power grid is costly to reach or seen as unreliable. Here, a well designed off-grid system can cost the same as, or less than, a grid connection. It comes with the benefit of producing power for free and keeping the lights (and fridge) on in a blackout."]},{"k":"p","s":["As one participant said:"]},{"k":"p","s":["my goal was to be self-sufficient […] generating our own power, sourcing our own water and dealing with our own waste. So in other words, I’m not reliant on any of the government utilities."]},{"k":"p","s":["One downside was the cost of replacement. Household batteries may need to be replaced every ",["ten to 15 years","https://www.choice.com.au/home-improvement/energy-saving/solar/buying-guides/battery-storage-buying-guide"],". As another respondent said:"]},{"k":"p","s":["the return on investment is 20 years and that battery will probably be dead by then."]},{"k":"p","s":["Only 20% of our respondents went off-grid out of necessity. Almost all the tiny-house residents we spoke with chose this way of living to suit their environmental values."]},{"k":"h","s":["Streamlining rules makes sense"]},{"k":"p","s":["Off-grid living isn’t for everyone. For suburban households, ",["research shows","https://doi.org/10.1016/j.apenergy.2019.03.068"]," it’s usually cheaper to add solar but keep the grid connection. Adding a battery as well can bring most of the benefits of going off-grid, such as ",["lower bills and blackout backup","https://doi.org/10.1016/j.rser.2026.117270"],", while keeping the grid as insurance."]},{"k":"p","s":["Going off-grid makes most sense on rural blocks where connection costs are very high, as well as in disaster-prone areas where long blackouts are possible."]},{"k":"p","s":["The biggest barrier is whether the rules will allow it. It’s often unclear whether a home can disconnect from electricity, let alone water and sewerage. The answer changes from council to council – and energy companies have their own rules on top."]},{"k":"p","s":["This situation means many householders are uncertain about what they’re legally allowed to do – especially the residents of tiny houses."]},{"k":"p","s":["Several told us they were flying under the radar and keeping their home out of sight from the road because they weren’t sure their homes were permitted to be there."]},{"k":"p","s":["To make it viable for more residents, three changes would help:"]},{"k":"li","s":["Make rules consistent state-wide, with clear pathways for both fixed houses and tiny houses on wheels."]},{"k":"li","s":["Ensure householders can access independent advice so that solar and battery systems are sized properly."]},{"k":"li","s":["Collect better data on off-grid households so authorities can plan for their needs."]},{"k":"p","s":["For a growing number of people in Queensland and around Australia, going off-grid is a real choice. Governments should plan for it in their energy and housing policies."]}],"chars":5687}};
